On Jun 12, 4:53=A0pm, Jistan Idiot <jistanid...@gmail.com> wrote:
> So I've got a level map that's 74 chars wide.
>
> When I put it into the game, it adds a 75th column (in front of the
> map, on the left).

     Maze-style levels are required to have even or odd size (I don't
recall which) so that the code that places stairs and other things
can deduce which rows and columns contain walls and which don't.  If
a map starts out with the wrong "parity" then it gets padded by one
column and/or one row to force that to be right.  Room-style and
cavern-style levels don't care about this.

> If I define the map with 75 columns in the des file, I get some error
> about the map being too big.

     From lev_comp when processing x.des into x.lev, or from nethack
when trying to load x.lev?  The width should be able to reach 77 or
78 columns before you start running into trouble.

> Is this intentional (if so why)? What do I do if I want to define the
> whole playing area?

     One thing to keep in mind is that lev_comp treats trailing
spaces within MAP...ENDMAP sections as significant rather than as
just whitespace, so your map might actually be wider than you think
if there are excess spaces present in your .des file.


